    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d212-a1">

                <pb facs="0364" n="298" sameAs="frus:frus1931-41v02/pg298"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Enclosure]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Oral Statement by the American Ambassador</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Japanese
                        Minister for Foreign A fairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Matsuoka</hi>)<anchor n="14" xml:id="d212-f1"/></head>
                <p>I am informed by our Consul at Hanoi that certain merchandise owned by American
                    interests is being refused re-export permits from <pb facs="0365" n="299"
                        xml:id="pg299"/> Indo-China by the Indo-Chinese authorities chieflly as a
                    result of Japanese pressure brought upon those authorities.</p>
                <p>Detailed information regarding such merchandise is known to the Indo-China
                    authorities, to our Consul, and presumably to the Japanese.</p>
                <p>I have been instructed to request that appropriate steps be taken to put an end
                    to this unwarranted interference with the shipments of goods and merchandise
                    owned by Americans.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d212-f1" n="14" type="source" xml:id="d212-f1-content">
                <p>Notation: “Left with Mr. Terazaki, Director of the American Bureau of the
                    Japanese Foreign Office, by the American First Secretary, Mr. Crocker, November
                    15, 1940, as from the American Ambassador to the Minister for Foreign
                    Affairs”.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
